    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now give a client a standard discount percentage, and any new invoice for that client starts with that discount already on it. The e2e test passes: an invoice for a client with a 10% standard discount on $200 of work shows a $20.00 discount and a $180.00 total.\n\nThe request mentioned an \"OfficeFloor server\", but the server here is ordinary Spring Boot (only the package name says officefloor), so I followed the Spring conventions in `CLAUDE.md`.\n\n**Database:** `V52__client_default_discount.sql` adds a `default_discount_pct` column to clients. It defaults to 0, so existing clients have no standard discount.\n\n**Server:**\n- The client record now stores the standard discount. It's included when reading a client and can be set when adding or editing one, as long as it's between 0 and 100.\n- When a new invoice is created and the client's standard discount is above 0, that percentage is added to the invoice as a regular discount. After that it can be edited or removed like any other discount.\n- Changing a client's standard discount doesn't touch invoices that already exist, drafts included. That's a judgement call: the tax settings, by contrast, do rework existing drafts when they change.\n- The test setup endpoint (`TestSupportController`) accepts `defaultDiscountPct` on clients. A test invoice that doesn't specify any discounts gets the client's standard discount, the same way a newly created invoice would.\n\n**Front end:**\n- The add-client and edit-client forms have a new \"Standard discount %\" field, test IDs `client-form-default-discount` and `client-edit-form-default-discount`. A value outside 0\u2013100 shows an error message.\n- The client detail page shows the standard discount (`client-default-discount`), or \"None\" if there isn't one.\n- No existing `data-testid` was renamed or removed.",
